id: pm-slack-standup-011l
title: "Standup read failure must refuse, not render an empty standup"
description: "fetchAllItems returns [] on any pm list-all failure, so pm standup export exits 0 with an empty standup indistinguishable from a quiet day. Match the fleet convention (pm-jira/pm-todos): throw a CommandError naming the exit status, stderr, and ENOBUFS buffer ceiling. Resolve the project's own @unbrained/pm-cli pm binary instead of PATH, and pass an explicit 64 MiB maxBuffer."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 2
tags: []
created_at: "2026-08-14T05:34:16.571Z"
updated_at: "2026-08-15T15:23:44.137Z"
closed_at: "2026-08-14T05:49:48.501Z"
completed_at: "2026-08-14T05:49:48.501Z"
claim_principal: pi-agent
author: pi-agent
resolution: "fetchAllItems throws a CommandError on every pm read failure instead of returning an empty array, resolves the project-local pm binary, passes an explicit maxBuffer and a read timeout, and names the exit status."
expected_result: "pm standup export exits non-zero when the underlying read fails, rather than exiting 0 with an empty standup."
actual_result: "Verified end to end: the failing read now aborts with a non-zero exit; six of sixteen tests fail when the fix is reverted."
comments[3]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-14T05:43:43.033Z",pi-agent,"Implemented: fetchAllItems now throws CommandError on any pm list-all failure (non-zero status, spawn error, ENOBUFS overrun, unparseable JSON) instead of degrading to []. Added pmJsonMaxBuffer (64 MiB, PM_JSON_MAX_BUFFER override) and describePmReadFailure matching the pm-jira/pm-todos convention, resolvePmBin to use the project-local @unbrained/pm-cli pm shim with PATH fallback, and an explicit maxBuffer. 16 new tests cover each failure shape, binary resolution, and a command-path regression test (runExporter) proving pm standup export exits non-zero on a failed read. Revert experiment confirmed the regression test fails (Missing expected rejection) with the old return [] restored."
  "2026-08-15T14:24:30.381Z",claude-agent,"Review round 2 (CodeRabbit on PR #35) — all three findings adopted, and one of them was larger than reported.\n\nADOPTED — `envelope.items ?? []` accepted any JSON value under `items`. A `{\"items\":{}}` response is zero-exit and well-formed, so nothing about the process outcome distinguishes it from success; the non-array flowed through as `PmItem[]` and failed inside `buildStandupData` with a TypeError naming neither the command nor the payload. Every other malformed shape in this reader is refused with a `CommandError` at the read, and now so is this one. Regression test added; reverting the guard fails it.\n\nADOPTED, wider than reported — CodeRabbit noted the preflight scope declared `commands: [\"standup\"]` while `slack-standup` is registered as a full command sharing `runStandupCommand` (not a Commander alias), so the runtime's exact-normalized-name match left the alias outside the override. Correct. Deriving the expected list from the real activation rather than trusting the review text showed a **third** path also outside the scope: `standup export`. The scope now names all three. The override is a pass-through today so nothing misbehaved at runtime, but the declared scope is this package's ownership claim as `pm health` reads it, and an authoritative override would have let two of three paths escape their gate. The new test derives the expectation from `activation.commands.handlers`, so a newly registered path fails here instead of quietly falling outside the scope.\n\nADOPTED — every fake `pm` binary in `test/fetch-all-items.test.ts` is a `#!/bin/sh` script and the timeout case uses `sleep 30`, so on the `windows-acceptance-launcher` job those spawns fail for reasons unrelated to the behaviour under test and the failure names the wrong cause. Nine tests now carry a `posixOnly` skip. The win32 launch path itself stays covered on every platform by the `resolvePmBin` cmd.exe tests, which do not spawn.\n\nVerified: all suites pass, `skipped 0` on this POSIX host, `release:check` green including `changelog:check`.\n"
  "2026-08-15T15:23:44.137Z",claude-agent,"Review round 3 (Greptile P1 on PR #35) — adopted, with the fix shaped to what cmd.exe actually permits.\n\nFINDING — the win32 launch composes a `cmd /c` tail, and `quoteWindowsArg` neutralizes every metacharacter cmd honours inside quotes EXCEPT `%`. cmd expands `%NAME%` even within a quoted string and there is no escape for it on a command tail. The code already documented that limit, but resolved it with an assumption: \"no path this package launches is expected to contain one\". That assumption is wrong, because a Windows workspace path is user-chosen and `--pm-path` carries it straight into this tail. `C:\\work\\%BUILD%\\pm` silently becomes whatever `%BUILD%` expands to, or nothing.\n\nThe consequence is the exact failure class the rest of this PR closes: pm reads a DIFFERENT workspace, and the standup is built from it while reporting success. A quiet wrong answer, not a crash.\n\nFIX — the expansion cannot be prevented, so the failure is made loud. `assertNoCmdVariableExpansion` refuses the launch and names the offending argument, explaining what proceeding would have cost. Only a `%...%` PAIR is refused, since only a pair can name a variable — an ordinary literal percent (`C:\\reports\\100% done`) still launches, because refusing that would break valid workspaces to guard against a case cmd.exe does not expand.\n\nThe documented \"known limit\" comment was rewritten: the limit is real, but it is no longer left to chance.\n\nTwo tests: one asserts the refusal names `%BUILD%` and the cost, the other asserts a literal percent survives into the tail. Verified non-vacuous: removing the guard makes the refusal test fail.\n\nBoth carry the `posixOnly` guard's inverse property — they call `pmLaunchPlan(bin, \"win32\")` directly rather than spawning, so they run on every platform including the POSIX CI hosts, and do not depend on the windows-acceptance-launcher job to be exercised.\n\nrelease:check green.\n"
files[3]{path,scope}:
  index.ts,project
  test/fetch-all-items.test.ts,project
